<LearningGuideLayout title="How to Change Scenes and Load Levels in Godot" description="Change complete levels or stream sub-scenes in Godot 4 while handling fades, cleanup, persistent state, and player control." summary="A level transition is more than loading a file. The old level must release its audio, cameras, triggers, and temporary state while the new level establishes a valid spawn point and returns control at the right moment." relatedSlugs={["build-levels-in-blender-for-godot","godot-trigger-system","play-3d-audio-godot"]} godotSource={{label:"SceneTree change_scene_to_file reference",href:"https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/classes/class_scenetree.html#class-scenetree-method-change-scene-to-file"}}>
    <div class="guide-summary"><strong>First decision:</strong> replace the whole level when its lifetime is finished; load a sub-scene when the parent level, player, or shared systems must remain active.</div>
    <h2>Choose the ownership boundary</h2>
    <ul><li><strong>Full scene change:</strong> separate levels, menus, or chapters with clean ownership boundaries.</li><li><strong>Instanced sub-scene:</strong> interiors, streamed regions, overlays, encounters, or reusable spaces inside a persistent level.</li><li><strong>Persistent manager:</strong> save data, settings, music policy, and transition state that must outlive either scene.</li></ul>
    <h2>A safe transition sequence</h2>
    <ol><li>Reject duplicate transition requests immediately.</li><li>Disable player input and display a fade or loading state.</li><li>Save only durable state: IDs, inventory, flags, and destination—not live node references.</li><li>Stop or transfer owned audio, timers, and async work.</li><li>Load the destination and wait until its required nodes are ready.</li><li>Place the player at a stable spawn ID, remove the fade, and restore input.</li></ol>
    <h2>Stream a sub-scene</h2>
    <p>Instantiate the packed scene beneath a dedicated host and keep the returned instance as the authority for unloading it. Prevent a doorway trigger from creating the same interior twice. When unloading, ensure no persistent manager keeps references to nodes inside the removed scene.</p>
    <h2>Common mistakes</h2>
    <ul><li>Allowing a trigger to fire repeatedly while the first load is still running.</li><li>Keeping node references across a full scene replacement.</li><li>Restoring controls before the destination and spawn point are ready.</li><li>Leaving music, cameras, or overlays owned by the old level alive.</li><li>Using filenames as spawn identity instead of an explicit destination ID.</li></ul>
    <Fragment slot="fpe"><p>Use <strong>Load Level</strong> when FPE should unload the current GLB and clear its feature registries before opening another level. Use a Blender-authored <strong>Sub Scene</strong> host for interiors or streamed regions that retain the parent. Pair doorway triggers with load/unload commands, and use control, camera, animation, and fade commands to make the handoff deliberate.</p><p><a href="/docs/fpe-subscenes-and-levels.html">Read the complete FPE level and sub-scene recipes</a>.</p></Fragment>
</LearningGuideLayout>
